<html>
<title>Switch Mnemonic</title>
<body>


<h1>Switch Mnemonic</h1>


<h2>Opcodes</h2>

<p>
There are a few CDP1802 opcodes with multiple mnemonics:
</p>
<ul>
<li><code>33</code>: <code>BDF</code>, <code>BPZ</code>, or <code>BGE</code></li>
<li><code>38</code>: <code>NBR</code> or <code>SKP</code></li>
<li><code>3B</code>: <code>BNF</code>, <code>BM</code>, or <code>BL</code></li>
<li><code>76</code>: <code>SHRC</code> or <code>RSHR</code></li>
<li><code>7E</code>: <code>SHLC</code> or <code>RSHL</code></li>
<li><code>C8</code>: <code>NLBR</code> or <code>LSKP</code></li>
</ul>

<p>
All variants are accepted in the assembler. The variant used will be stored as the memory type. This can also be changed via the procedure described in the <a href="DirectMarking.htm">Memory Type Selection</a> section, using the <code>Switch</code> marking type. When no type is specified, the first mnemonic listed for each opcode will be used.
</p>


<h2>Example</h2>

<p>
For example, if the type is set to <code>Switch</code> (1) and the <strong>Mark</strong> button (2) is pressed on address 0000 (hexadecimal) in the following example:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <code>Switch</code> type selection</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Mark</strong> button</li>
</ul>

<p>
the code will change to:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark_result.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Press <strong>Mark</strong> again on address 0000 (hexadecimal) and the code will change to:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark_result2.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</body>
</html>
